.TH RIVET 1
.SH NAME
rivet \- a girder climber
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/rivet
[
.B -t
|
.B -s
] [
.B -q
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rivet
is a climbing game in the shape the arcade settled on in 1981:
a stack of girders joined by ladders,
hazards that roll along them and fall from one to the next in a zigzag,
a mallet that turns those hazards into points for a few seconds,
and one thing at the top to reach.
Reaching it scores, advances the level and makes the next set of
hazards faster.
Being touched by one costs a life; three lives and the game is over.
Jumping clean over a hazard scores 100.
.PP
The controls are the pad
.IR nes (2)
defines: the arrow keys or
.B wasd
to walk and to climb a ladder,
.BR x ,
.B .
or the space bar to jump,
.B q
or Escape to quit.
Up and down work a ladder when standing on one.
.PP
The level is written as thirty strings of thirty-two characters at the
top of the source, and the direction a hazard rolls along each platform
is a property of the map rather than of the hazard, which is what makes
the zigzag come out.
.PP
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test described below instead of playing, and needs no
window.
.B -s
plays a scripted opening headless and writes the composed frame to
.B rivet.raw
in the current directory as
.IR w " by " h
of blue, green, red bytes, which is how the screenshot in
.B doc/
was made.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
games/rivet
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/rivet.dis \-t
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/rivet.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nes (2),
.IR nestest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B -t
drives the same
.I step
the game loop drives, with the same pad, and asserts on game state
rather than on pixels \- a count of lit pixels cannot tell a climber
walking from one stuck against a wall.
It checks the map's shape, that walking moves one pixel a frame and
stops at the wall, that a jump returns to the row it left, that a ladder
is climbed and descended, that a hazard rolls the way its platform says
and turns round on the one it lands on, that contact costs a life and
that contact with the mallet up scores instead, and that reaching the
top advances the level and speeds the hazards up.
It draws every frame too, so a fault in the drawing is still a crash
here.
The last line is
.B PASSED
or
.BR FAILED .
.SH BUGS
There is one level layout.
Later levels differ only in how fast and how often the hazards come.
.PP
The self test asserts on state, so a change that broke the drawing
without crashing \- a sprite in the wrong palette, a background never
blitted \- would pass it.
.B -s
and a look at the result is the check for that, and it is a manual one.
.PP
A hazard that lands exactly on the climber counts as a hit even if the
climber is climbing, where the arcade would have let a ladder be a
refuge.
